{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 99,
    "dDurationMs": 4691,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Milky Way galaxy is relatively calm by\nthe destructive standards of the rest of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4790,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Universe, and compared to its own very violent\npast."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8270,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But just recently we discovered that its violent\npast was much more recent than we thought"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13790,
    "dDurationMs": 3490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- and could even happen again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21440,
    "dDurationMs": 3710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You may be wondering why I'm not floating\nsomewhere off in space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25150,
    "dDurationMs": 4730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You'd think it would be the perfect place\nto weather a global pandemic."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29880,
    "dDurationMs": 4510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not so - like many other people, I'm confined\nto my real world apartment for the duration"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34390,
    "dDurationMs": 4469,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- but I promise that won't stop us bringing\nyou the wonders of the cosmos."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38859,
    "dDurationMs": 5230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Whatever is happening here on Earth, the universe\nremains awesome."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44089,
    "dDurationMs": 3711,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, the center of the Milky Way galaxy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47800,
    "dDurationMs": 5009,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not only is it home to an enormous black hole\nfour million times the mass of the Sun, but"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52809,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it also swarms with smaller black holes, searing\nhot clouds of gas, massive stars right on"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58569,
    "dDurationMs": 5230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the edge of going supernova, and some of the\nmost energetic radiation in the cosmos."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63799,
    "dDurationMs": 4591,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yet as extreme as it may sound, the present-day\nMilky Way is actually relatively calm by the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68390,
    "dDurationMs": 2590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "standards of many other galaxies in the Universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70980,
    "dDurationMs": 6309,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our central black hole, Sagittarius A*, has\nbeen in a quiet or “inactive” phase for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77289,
    "dDurationMs": 1711,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as long as we’ve been observing it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79000,
    "dDurationMs": 4990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But across the Universe, there are loads of\n“active” galaxies - ones that harbour"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 83990,
    "dDurationMs": 4949,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "active galactic nuclei, or AGNs, at their\ncenters."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88939,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In these, the supermassive black hole at the\ngalactic core is in the process of sucking"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93759,
    "dDurationMs": 3220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "down a blazing hot vortex of gas."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96979,
    "dDurationMs": 4861,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The most powerful AGNs are called quasars,\nand they can shine a thousand times brighter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101840,
    "dDurationMs": 2190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than their surrounding galaxy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104030,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve talked quasars — and what makes\nthem so awesome — in a previous episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109530,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Most researchers in the field believe that\nall supermassive black holes went through"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113810,
    "dDurationMs": 6129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "violent AGN phases in the past — and that\nincludes our very own Sagittarius A*."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119939,
    "dDurationMs": 4701,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Milky Way was probably once an AGN - and\nit’s just as well for us that this activity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124640,
    "dDurationMs": 2089,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is long past."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126729,
    "dDurationMs": 1521,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or is it?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128250,
    "dDurationMs": 5561,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "According to a series of recent studies, Sag\nA*’s last feeding binge may have been more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133811,
    "dDurationMs": 5219,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "recent that we thought - and it’s appetite\nmay even be waking up again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139030,
    "dDurationMs": 7690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll start in 2010, when a team of astronomers\nfrom the Harvard-Smithsonian Centre for Astrophysics"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146720,
    "dDurationMs": 5430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were using the Fermi Gamma-ray Space Telescope\nto look for evidence of dark matter in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152150,
    "dDurationMs": 1700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "innermost regions of the Milky Way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 153850,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "See, astronomers believe that when dark matter\nparticles crash into and annihilate each other,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158950,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the result could be a fireworks show of high-energy\ngamma rays."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162670,
    "dDurationMs": 5850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We have of course covered dark matter and\ndark energy in detail previously."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168520,
    "dDurationMs": 6020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before I get to what the team observed, let\nme describe what they expected to see."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174540,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dark matter has the property that it tends\nto spread out diffusely and evenly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178900,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the case of the Milky Way it should form\na vast ball that envelops the galaxy, thickening"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184090,
    "dDurationMs": 2590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "slightly towards the center."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186680,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gamma rays produced by dark matter annihilations\nshould have similar structure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191600,
    "dDurationMs": 2440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that is NOT what the team saw."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194040,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They found something totally unexpected, and\narguably just as amazing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 198860,
    "dDurationMs": 6150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Instead of a diffuse cloud of gamma rays,\nthey saw a massive pair of high-energy gamma"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205010,
    "dDurationMs": 5390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ray bubbles with sharp edges extending more\nthan 25,000 light years in either direction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 210400,
    "dDurationMs": 4611,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "above and below the plane of the Milky Way\n- that’ nearly a third the width of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215011,
    "dDurationMs": 5279,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entire Milky Way disk — and somehow, no\none had ever noticed it before."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220290,
    "dDurationMs": 2580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We call these the “Fermi Bubbles”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222870,
    "dDurationMs": 4170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How does something so massive and so powerful\nstay hidden for so long?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227040,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, it turns out that the plane of the Galaxy\nalso glows brightly in gamma rays."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232400,
    "dDurationMs": 3890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is mostly from cosmic ray interactions\nwith the interstellar medium."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236290,
    "dDurationMs": 6470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It goes like this: atomic nuclei - mostly\nlone protons - can get accelerated to extreme"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242760,
    "dDurationMs": 3820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energies, typically in supernovae or other\ncataclysmic events."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246580,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These “cosmic rays” can then collide with\nnuclei in the gas between the stars - again,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253120,
    "dDurationMs": 2230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mostly the protons of hydrogen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255350,
    "dDurationMs": 7030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The protons sometimes obliterate each other\nto form a neutral pion particle plus some other stuff."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262380,
    "dDurationMs": 3740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The neutral pion will then decay into a pair\nof gamma rays."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266130,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that’s the glow you see when you look\nat the disk of the Milky Way - if you have"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270320,
    "dDurationMs": 1450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gamma ray vision."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271770,
    "dDurationMs": 6890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This gamma ray fog was masking the mysterious\nbubbles lurking in the background."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278660,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Unless you know how to look that is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281000,
    "dDurationMs": 5900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The key to finding the Fermi Bubbles was that\nits gamma rays are even more energetic than"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286900,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the diffuse emission."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 289120,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The gamma rays produced by neutral pion decay\nfrom these cosmic ray collisions tend to drop"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294640,
    "dDurationMs": 2510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "off in intensity towards higher energies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297150,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, fewer very high energy gamma rays."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300330,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand, the Fermi Bubbles produce\na lot more gamma rays at higher energies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305610,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The difference in energy distributions - or\nin their gamma ray spectra, means that the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311250,
    "dDurationMs": 4210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "diffuse gamma ray background can be cleanly\nsubtracted."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 315460,
    "dDurationMs": 3350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And voila, the Fermi Bubbles shine through."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318810,
    "dDurationMs": 4950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the Fermi Bubbles, gamma rays are generated\nby a high-energy process known as Inverse"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323760,
    "dDurationMs": 1370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Compton Scattering."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325130,
    "dDurationMs": 3970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now that’s pretty clear from the shape of the\nspectrum alone."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329100,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Extremely energetic electrons interact with\nlower-energy light, boosting that light to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334200,
    "dDurationMs": 1939,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the much more energetic gamma ray regime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336139,
    "dDurationMs": 3926,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And so that’s what we’re seeing here - light\nbounced off extremely"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340065,
    "dDurationMs": 2355,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "high energy electrons within the vast bubbles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342420,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s estimated that the energy contained\nin this ocean of electrons is equivalent to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348200,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that released by 100,000 supernova explosions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 352940,
    "dDurationMs": 5090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the questions become - where the bleep\ndid that energy come from, and when was it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358030,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "released?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359030,
    "dDurationMs": 3730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s start with “when” - it’s a bit\nmore straightforward."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362760,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Based on the speed of the gas within the bubbles\n-measured at nearly 9000 km/s by the Hubble"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367460,
    "dDurationMs": 4959,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Space Telescope - the bubbles must have been\ngrowing for a few million years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372419,
    "dDurationMs": 4261,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Basically it happened yesterday - at least\ncompared to the 10 billion year"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376680,
    "dDurationMs": 1280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "age of the Milky Way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377960,
    "dDurationMs": 3310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But exactly what happened a few million years\nago?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381270,
    "dDurationMs": 5790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mentioned an energy of 100,000 supernovae\n- well that’s actually a serious option."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387060,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Option number 2 is more terrifying: a temper\ntantrum from Sagittarius A*."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 393580,
    "dDurationMs": 2830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s look at the first scenario."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396410,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Believe it or not, it’s not so uncommon\nfor astronomers to witness storms of supernova"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 400630,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "explosions raging across a galaxy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403570,
    "dDurationMs": 3770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or at least to catch a snapshot of a galaxy\nin the middle of such an event."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407340,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When a galaxy has a lot of fresh gas and when\nthat gas gets a shake - for example, if the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412650,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "galaxy is shaken by a collision or close interaction\nwith another galaxy - then that gas can form"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418710,
    "dDurationMs": 5910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stars at a really insane rate across the galaxy. We call these events starbursts"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424620,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and we see them in many other galaxies like M82 and the Antennae Galaxy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429540,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The star formation part of a starburst isn’t\nenergetic enough to produce the Fermi Bubbles,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434280,
    "dDurationMs": 5130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but starbursts are always accompanied by an\nenormous number of supernova explosions because"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439410,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the most massive stars produced in the starburst\ndie - rather explosively - very quickly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445830,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The shock waves created by the supernovae\nfrom a starburst in the Milky Way could literally"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451350,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "punch a hole through the interstellar medium,\npotentially resulting in something like the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 456190,
    "dDurationMs": 3650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "massive bubble-shaped cavities we see today."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459840,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well that sounds promising — but there’s a problem."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463120,
    "dDurationMs": 4710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All of those supernovae would have had to\nleave remnants behind - neutron stars and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467830,
    "dDurationMs": 1450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469280,
    "dDurationMs": 5531,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The neutron stars should be seen as pulsars,\nand there just aren’t enough in that region"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 474811,
    "dDurationMs": 3389,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to account for a starburst of the required\nmagnitude."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478200,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so what about option 2?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 481020,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Could the Fermi Bubbles have been created\nby activity from our supermassive black hole?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 485800,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, could Milky Way have been a full-blown\nAGN just a few million years ago?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 491020,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To answer that we need to consider how active\nthe black hole would have to have been."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 496060,
    "dDurationMs": 5810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now A full quasar might devour many millions of\ntimes the mass of the Sun over one active"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 501870,
    "dDurationMs": 3700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "period, which could last for several million\nyears."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 505570,
    "dDurationMs": 6129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A lot of that mass goes into the black hole,\nbut 10% or more is converted into energy in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511699,
    "dDurationMs": 5830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the form of light before it hits the black\nhole, which is why quasars shine so bright."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517529,
    "dDurationMs": 4771,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sagittarius A* would have converted matter\nto energy with much less efficiency."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522300,
    "dDurationMs": 7069,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nonetheless, it’s estimated that to energize\nthe Fermi Bubbles, Sag A* would have needed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529369,
    "dDurationMs": 6551,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to devour - a single 50 solar mass star that\nhappened to wander too close."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535920,
    "dDurationMs": 5050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So it would have been less of a feast and\nmore of a snack followed by a burp."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540970,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Definitely not enough to turn Sag A* into\na quasar."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544490,
    "dDurationMs": 4250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But even that single relatively small event\ncould have driven jets in the directions of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548740,
    "dDurationMs": 1469,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "those bubbles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550209,
    "dDurationMs": 2701,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That all sound good in terms of our energy\nbudget."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 552910,
    "dDurationMs": 3690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Unfortunately, there’s a problem with this\ntheory too."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556600,
    "dDurationMs": 5450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With jets alone, there’s no way to carve\nout structure quite like these bubbles from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 562050,
    "dDurationMs": 1310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the interstellar medium."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 563360,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AGN jets that get trapped within their galaxies\ntend to form more wedge-like shaped structures"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 569540,
    "dDurationMs": 3659,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with internal structure - for example hotspots."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 573199,
    "dDurationMs": 6740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the Fermi Bubbles are … well, bubbly,\nand they have relatively uniform intensity - they’re"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579939,
    "dDurationMs": 3161,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "much smoother than we would be expected from\na jet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 583100,
    "dDurationMs": 3830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what - two nice theories and neither of\nthem work?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586930,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Actually, both of them work - if they work\ntogether."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 590050,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here is our best thinking on the most likely\nscenario."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 594490,
    "dDurationMs": 6209,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A mini AGN phase is triggered either by an\ninflux of gas or by a random massive star"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600699,
    "dDurationMs": 2010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "getting too close to the black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 602709,
    "dDurationMs": 6021,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A pair of powerful jets and/or winds blast\nout from the Galactic Centre, and any remaining"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608730,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gas along the path of these outflows would\nbe compressed by shocks, provoking a sudden"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 614550,
    "dDurationMs": 3129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "burst of star formation like we described\npreviously."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617679,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This starburst and the subsequent supernova\nbarrage smooths out the energy in the bubbles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623959,
    "dDurationMs": 6820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AGN activity and starburst activity are very\ncommonly seen together - in fact M82 that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630779,
    "dDurationMs": 3541,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we saw previously harbours an AGN in its core."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634320,
    "dDurationMs": 5459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The energy from those outflows will eventually\nshut down the star formation, but in the early"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639779,
    "dDurationMs": 2161,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "phase it can help kick it off."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641940,
    "dDurationMs": 2179,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so problem solved?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644119,
    "dDurationMs": 2590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well maybe - this is still just our best guess."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646709,
    "dDurationMs": 4301,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But new studies are unlocking the secrets\nof the galactic core."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 651010,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a result published last year in Nature,\na team of astronomers using South Africa’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 656180,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "MeerKAT telescope array discovered a handful\nof never-before-seen radio structures throughout"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661430,
    "dDurationMs": 5439,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Galaxy — including two bubble-shaped\nstructures extending above and below the Galactic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 666869,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "plane."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 667869,
    "dDurationMs": 4970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just like the Fermi Bubbles, these radio bubbles\nseem to originate from our central black hole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672840,
    "dDurationMs": 3380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and extend in opposite directions above and\nbelow."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676220,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They’re also likely generated by electrons,\nbut in this case, the electrons are accelerated"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 681420,
    "dDurationMs": 4419,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by magnetic fields and are emitting what’s known\nas synchrotron radiation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 685839,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The biggest difference, however, is that these\nparticular structures only extend 1400 light"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690749,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "years above and below the plane of the Galaxy\n— leading astronomers to believe that they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695449,
    "dDurationMs": 5601,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "might be younger, more recent versions of\nthe much-larger Fermi Bubbles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 701050,
    "dDurationMs": 4399,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although the energy required to power these\nradio bubbles is several thousand times lower"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705449,
    "dDurationMs": 4550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than that required for the Fermi Bubbles,\nevidence points to similar explanations in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709999,
    "dDurationMs": 5361,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "both cases — some combination of accretion\nonto the Milky Way’s central black hole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715360,
    "dDurationMs": 3449,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and a flurry of star formation egging each\nother on."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 718809,
    "dDurationMs": 5650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This was a relatively weak event, but it was\nalso relatively recent."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724460,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So do we need to be worried about it happening\nagain?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729500,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well we probably don’t need to agonize over any\nextra gamma rays being generated by this process,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735929,
    "dDurationMs": 5861,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because our Earth’s atmosphere absorbs most\nof the gamma ray radiation that comes our way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741790,
    "dDurationMs": 5900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However researchers believe that the starburst\nand accompanying supernova cascade that may"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 747690,
    "dDurationMs": 4949,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have helped forged the Fermi Bubbles would\nhave been visible to the naked eye for our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752639,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "early hominid ancestors."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 755199,
    "dDurationMs": 5171,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The night sky several million years ago may\nhave been much brighter than we ever could"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 760370,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have imagined."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 761370,
    "dDurationMs": 3079,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We may have to wait until something like this\nhappens again to know for sure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 764449,
    "dDurationMs": 2080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That could take a while."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 766529,
    "dDurationMs": 1250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or will it?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767779,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a paper posted last month, a team of astronomers\npresented evidence that the X-ray activity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772899,
    "dDurationMs": 3591,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of our central black hole has been increasing\nover the past four years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 776490,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While this probably doesn’t mean that we’re\ngoing to witness the creation of a newborn"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779730,
    "dDurationMs": 4770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fermi Bubbles in the near future, we may be able to get new insights into the all-too"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784500,
    "dDurationMs": 5569,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "frequent tantrums of Sagittarius A*, and their\nsurprisingly large influence on the Milky"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790069,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Way - a not-so-inactive galactic denizen of\nspace time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795689,
    "dDurationMs": 1250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey Everyone."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 796939,
    "dDurationMs": 4650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we do comments, if you're looking for\nmore science content to help get you through"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 801589,
    "dDurationMs": 1481,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all that's going on."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803070,
    "dDurationMs": 4459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "PBS has a lot of great shows including Antarctic\nExtremes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 807529,
    "dDurationMs": 5381,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s a journey to Earth’s most remote\nlaboratories to see how science is done in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 812910,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Antartica."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813910,
    "dDurationMs": 5630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s out now on PBS's new science channel\nTerra and there's a link in the description."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 819540,
    "dDurationMs": 7079,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And speaking of Terra, last week we delved into the science and non-science of how we determine the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 826620,
    "dDurationMs": 2360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "age of the earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828980,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First up, several people were offended that\nI didn’t actually mention Clair Patterson,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834420,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who was the first person to use uranium-lead\ndating with zircon crystals to calculate the age of the Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 841100,
    "dDurationMs": 4579,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now we did give a review of the method, but\nyou guys are right that we should have mentioned"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 845679,
    "dDurationMs": 4791,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Clair – who was not only the first to get\nthe right age for the earth, significantly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 850470,
    "dDurationMs": 6179,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "advance this radiometric dating technique, but he\nalso discovered the disastrous lead levels"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 856649,
    "dDurationMs": 5261,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the environment due to industrial use of\nlead, and campaigned tirelessly against the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 861910,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "latter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 862910,
    "dDurationMs": 6270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Theo RixLux wants to know more about how cracks\nin a crystal can lead to a linear correlation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 869180,
    "dDurationMs": 2930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the concordance diagram."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 872110,
    "dDurationMs": 1180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sure thing, Theo."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873290,
    "dDurationMs": 8239,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So in a perfectly intact crystal, the ratios\nof lead-206 to uranium-238 on the Y-axis to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 881529,
    "dDurationMs": 5650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lead-207 to U-235 on the X-axis follow a curved\nline."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 887179,
    "dDurationMs": 6041,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The U-238 has a shorter half life so first\nit decays quickly in the first couple of billion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 893220,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "years, then the U-235 dominates the changes\nat later times, leading to the classing concordia"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 900120,
    "dDurationMs": 940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "curve."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 901060,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A number of you quoted Good Omens, which itself\nhas a very precise claim for the age of the Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 906060,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now I think I’ll just read the quote, because\nhow’m I gonna beat Terry Pratchett and Neil"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 910140,
    "dDurationMs": 2159,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gaiman for wit?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 912299,
    "dDurationMs": 3611,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\" Current theories on the creation of the\nUniverse state that, if it was created at"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 915910,
    "dDurationMs": 6729,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all and didn't just start, as it were, unofficially,\nit came into being between ten and twenty"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 922639,
    "dDurationMs": 2240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thousand million years ago."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 924879,
    "dDurationMs": 4490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the same token the earth itself is generally\nsupposed to be about four and a half thousand"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929369,
    "dDurationMs": 1761,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "million years old."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 931130,
    "dDurationMs": 2899,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These dates are incorrect."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 934029,
    "dDurationMs": 7710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Medieval Jewish scholars put the date of the\nCreation at 3760 B.C. Greek Orthodox theologians"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 941739,
    "dDurationMs": 3650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "put Creation as far back as 5508 B.C."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 945389,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These suggestions are also incorrect."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 948749,
    "dDurationMs": 6620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Archbishop James Usher (1580-1656) published\nAnnales Veteris et Novi Testamenti in 1654,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 955369,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which suggested that the Heaven and the Earth\nwere created in 4004 B.C."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 959749,
    "dDurationMs": 4981,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One of his aides took the calculation further,\nand was able to announce triumphantly that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 964730,
    "dDurationMs": 7889,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Earth was created on Sunday the 21th of\nOctober, 4004 B.C., at exactly 9:00 A.M.,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 972619,
    "dDurationMs": 5191,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because God liked to get work done early in\nthe morning while he was feeling fresh."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 977810,
    "dDurationMs": 2089,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This too was incorrect."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 979900,
    "dDurationMs": 2470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By almost a quarter of an hour.\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 982370,
    "dDurationMs": 5910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Tasha Montgomery points out that this makes\nEarth a Libra. Seriously Tasha"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 988280,
    "dDurationMs": 2580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Have you learned nothing on this show."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 990860,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A Libra? You forgot to take into account the precesion of the equinoxes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 995340,
    "dDurationMs": 6700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So accounting for our axil procession, I think that makes Earth a Taurus, which really explains so much."
    } ]
  } ]
}
